Shinonome bitmap font
---------------------
The embedded Unicode-indexed bitmap table is converted from the Shinonome
0.9.11p1 source archive, specifically `bdf/shnmk16.bdf` (16-dot JIS X
0208 Gothic) and `bdf/shnm8x16r.bdf` (16-dot JIS X 0201 ASCII and half-width
katakana):

https://openlab.jp/efont/dist/shinonome/shinonome-0.9.11p1.tar.bz2

The archive was designed by Yasuyuki Furukawa and maintained and distributed
by the Electronic Font Open Laboratory (/efont/). Its SHA-256 is
`95663c95c92ba5765f63ccbdf033eb93b707be01812a989c548db943479c838f`.
The conversion is reproducible with
`tools/src/bin/convert-shinonome.rs`; the BDF sources themselves are not
committed.

The source archive's `LICENSE` terms, translated into English, are:

> All font data, documentation, and scripts included in this archive are
> provided in the Public Domain. In Japan it is legally impossible at present
> to waive copyright; the authors listed in `AUTHORS` declare that they will
> not exercise those rights, making the work effectively Public Domain. Free
> modification, conversion to other formats, embedding, and redistribution are
> permitted. All of these are provided without warranty.

The Shinonome `k16` source notes that some symbol patterns originate from the
XFree86 `jiskanji16` font; that attribution is retained by using the complete
Shinonome source notice and its redistribution terms.

fmgen / rhythm attribution
--------------------------
The upstream PMDWin notice credits the rhythm engine lineage to cisc:

FM Sound Generator with OPN/OPM interface
Copyright (C) by cisc 1998, 2003.

The reference identifies this as fmgen 008, with PMDWin-specific changes. This
rewrite contains no fmgen source; its rhythm behavior is reimplemented from
the observed PMDWin behavior.

PMDWin / Professional Music Driver
----------------------------------
Professional Music Driver 4.8 was written by M. Kajihara and ported to Windows
by C60. The upstream reference identifies PMDWin as GPL version 2 and includes
additional attribution, source-disclosure, and contact conditions.

This repository does not include PMDWin source. The Rust implementation is a
clean-room reimplementation based on observed behavior. PMDWin's attribution
and applicable upstream conditions are recorded here for redistribution.
